      article.main
        h1 Attributes
        p Tag attributes look similar to HTML (with optional commas), but their values are just regular JavaScript.
        p
          | (NOTE: Examples on this page use the pipe character (
          code |
          | ) for
          a(href="plain-text.html#whitespace-control") whitespace control
          | .)
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="0")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-1872174260")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  a(href='//google.com') Google
                  |
                  |
                  a(class='button' href='//google.com') Google
                  |
                  |
                  a(class='button', href='//google.com') Google
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <a href="//google.com">Google</a>
                  <a class="button" href="//google.com">Google</a>
                  <a class="button" href="//google.com">Google</a>
        p Normal JavaScript expressions work fine, too:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="1")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-1070755619")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var authenticated = true
                  body(class=authenticated ? 'authed' : 'anon')
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <body class="authed"></body>
        h2#multiline-attributes
          | Multiline Attributes
          a.header-anchor(href="#multiline-attributes", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p If you have many attributes, you can also spread them across many lines:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="2")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1568381676")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  input(
                    type='checkbox'
                    name='agreement'
                    checked
                  )
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <input type="checkbox" name="agreement" checked="checked" />
        p
          | If your JavaScript runtime supports ES2015
          a(href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals") template strings
          | (including Node.js/io.js 1.0.0 and later), you can use that syntax for attributes. This is really useful for attributes with really long values:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="3")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-179719284")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  input(data-json=`
                    {
                      "very-long": "piece of ",
                      "data": true
                    }
                  `)
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <input data-json="
                    {
                      &quot;very-long&quot;: &quot;piece of &quot;,
                      &quot;data&quot;: true
                    }
                  " />
        h2#quoted-attributes
          | Quoted Attributes
          a.header-anchor(href="#quoted-attributes", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | If your attribute name contains odd characters that might interfere with JavaScript syntax, either quote it using
          code ""
          | or
          code ''
          | , or use commas to separate different attributes. Examples of such characters include
          code []
          | and
          code ()
          | (frequently used in Angular 2).
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="4")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1667884468")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  //- In this case, `(click)` is treated as a
                  //- function call instead of a attribute name,
                  //- resulting in the unusual error.
                  div(class='div-class' (click)='play()')
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  index.pug:4:11
                      2| //- function call instead of a attribute name,
                      3| //- resulting in the unusual error.
                    > 4| div(class='div-class' (click)='play()')
                  -----------------^
                  
                  Syntax Error: Assigning to rvalue
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="5")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1450229221")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  div(class='div-class', (click)='play()')
                  div(class='div-class' '(click)'='play()')
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <div class="div-class" (click)="play()"></div>
                  <div class="div-class" (click)="play()"></div>
        h2#attribute-interpolation
          | Attribute Interpolation
          a.header-anchor(href="#attribute-interpolation", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        div.alert.alert-danger
          h6 Caution
          p Previous versions of Pug/Jade supported an interpolation syntax such as:
          pre.cm-s-default
            code
              span.cm-tag a
              span.cm-punctuation (
              span.cm-attribute href
              span.cm-operator =
              span.cm-string "/#{url}"
              span.cm-punctuation )
              span.cm-string Link
          p
            | This syntax is
            strong no longer supported.
            | Alternatives are found below. (Check our
            a(href="../api/migration-v2.html") migration guide
            | for more information on other incompatibilities between Pug v2 and previous versions.)
        p Here are some alternatives you can use to include variables in your attribute:
        ol
          li
            p Simply write the attribute in JavaScript:
            div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="6")
              div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1906882382")
                div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
                  div(data-reactid="3")
                    textarea(data-reactid="4").
                      - var url = 'pug-test.html';
                      a(href='/' + url) Link
                      |
                      |
                      - url = 'https://example.com/'
                      a(href=url) Another link
                div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
                  div(data-reactid="6")
                    textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                      <a href="/pug-test.html">Link</a>
                      <a href="https://example.com/">Another link</a>
          li
            p
              | If your JavaScript runtime supports ES2015
              a(href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals") template strings
              | (including Node.js/io.js 1.0.0 and later), you can also use its syntax to simplify your attributes:
            div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="7")
              div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-575148380")
                div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
                  div(data-reactid="3")
                    textarea(data-reactid="4").
                      - var btnType = 'info'
                      - var btnSize = 'lg'
                      button(type='button' class='btn btn-' + btnType + ' btn-' + btnSize)
                      |
                      |
                      button(type='button' class=`btn btn-${btnType} btn-${btnSize}`)
                div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
                  div(data-reactid="6")
                    textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                      <button class="btn btn-info btn-lg" type="button"></button>
                      <button class="btn btn-info btn-lg" type="button"></button>
        h2#unescaped-attributes
          | Unescaped Attributes
          a.header-anchor(href="#unescaped-attributes", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | By default, all attributes are escaped—that is,special characters are replaced with escape sequences—to prevent attacks (such as cross site scripting). If you need to use special characters, use
          code !=
          | instead of
          code =
          | .
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="8")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-1114462472")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  div(escaped="<code>")
                  div(unescaped!="<code>")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <div escaped="&lt;code&gt;"></div>
                  <div unescaped="<code>"></div>
        div.alert.alert-danger
          h6 Caution
          p
            strong Unescaped buffered code can be dangerous.
            | You must be sure to sanitize any user inputs to avoid
            a(href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross-site_scripting") cross-site scripting
            | .
        h2#boolean-attributes
          | Boolean Attributes
          a.header-anchor(href="#boolean-attributes", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Boolean attributes are mirrored by Pug. Boolean values (
          code true
          | and
          code false
          | ) are accepted. When no value is specified
          code true
          | is assumed.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="9")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-2120286922")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  input(type='checkbox' checked)
                  |
                  |
                  input(type='checkbox' checked=true)
                  |
                  |
                  input(type='checkbox' checked=false)
                  |
                  |
                  input(type='checkbox' checked=true.toString())
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <input type="checkbox" checked="checked" />
                  <input type="checkbox" checked="checked" />
                  <input type="checkbox" />
                  <input type="checkbox" checked="true" />
        p
          | If the doctype is
          code html
          | , Pug knows not to mirror the attribute, and instead uses the terse style (understood by all browsers).
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="10")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="147783694")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  doctype html
                  |
                  |
                  input(type='checkbox' checked)
                  |
                  |
                  input(type='checkbox' checked=true)
                  |
                  |
                  input(type='checkbox' checked=false)
                  |
                  |
                  input(type='checkbox' checked=true && 'checked')
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <!DOCTYPE html>
                  <input type="checkbox" checked>
                  <input type="checkbox" checked>
                  <input type="checkbox">
                  <input type="checkbox" checked="checked">
        h2#style-attributes
          | Style Attributes
          a.header-anchor(href="#style-attributes", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | The
          code style
          | attribute can be a string, like any normal attribute; but it can also be an object, which is handy when styles are generated by JavaScript.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="11")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="838896838")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  a(style={color: 'red', background: 'green'})
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <a style="color:red;background:green;"></a>
        h2#class-attributes
          | Class Attributes
          a.header-anchor(href="#class-attributes", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | The
          code class
          | attribute can be a string, like any normal attribute; but it can also be an array of class names, which is handy when generated from JavaScript.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="12")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-1501576047")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var classes = ['foo', 'bar', 'baz']
                  a(class=classes)
                  |
                  |
                  //- the class attribute may also be repeated to merge arrays
                  a.bang(class=classes class=['bing'])
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <a class="foo bar baz"></a>
                  <a class="bang foo bar baz bing"></a>
        p
          | It can also be an object which maps class names to
          code true
          | or
          code false
          | values. This is useful for applying conditional classes
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="13")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="2000143768")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var currentUrl = '/about'
                  a(class={active: currentUrl === '/'} href='/') Home
                  |
                  |
                  a(class={active: currentUrl === '/about'} href='/about') About
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <a href="/">Home</a>
                  <a class="active" href="/about">About</a>
        h2#class-literal
          | Class Literal
          a.header-anchor(href="#class-literal", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Classes may be defined using a
          code .classname
          | syntax:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="14")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-356158522")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  a.button
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <a class="button"></a>
        p
          | Since
          code div
          | 's are such a common choice of tag, it is the default if you omit the tag name:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="15")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1213953511")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  .content
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <div class="content"></div>
        h2#id-literal
          | ID Literal
          a.header-anchor(href="#id-literal", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | IDs may be defined using a
          code #idname
          | syntax:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="16")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="256078906")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  a#main-link
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <a id="main-link"></a>
        p
          | Since
          code div
          | 's are such a common choice of tag, it is the default if you omit the tag name:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="17")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="-821529453")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  #content
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <div id="content"></div>
        h2#attributes
          | &attributes
          a.header-anchor(href="#attributes", aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Pronounced as “and attributes”, the
          code &attributes
          | syntax can be used to explode an object into attributes of an element.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="18")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="1245617865")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  div#foo(data-bar="foo")&attributes({'data-foo': 'bar'})
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly, data-reactid="7").
                  <div id="foo" data-bar="foo" data-foo="bar"></div>
        p
          | The above example uses an object literal. But you can also use a variable whose value is an object, too. (See also:
          a(href="mixins.html#mixin-attributes") Mixin Attributes
          | ).
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive", data-index="19")
          div.row(data-reactroot, data-reactid="1", data-react-checksum="968731221")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  - var attributes = {};
                  - attributes.class = 'baz';
                  div#foo(data-bar="foo")&attributes(attributes)
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
